{"reference":"Judges 20","book":"Judges","chapter":20,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"archaeology_geography","title":"Places in the Campaign","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter names Mizpah as the gathering place and Gibeah as the city against which Israel’s campaign is directed. Later movements include Beth-el, Baal-tamar, Maareh-geba, the rock of Rimmon, and Gidom. These names locate the assembly and fighting, but the account does not provide enough information to reconstruct a precise campaign route.","verse_refs":["Judges 20:1-3","Judges 20:19-20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_020_c8fd4341f372acd7bb30ed02"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Book’s Closing Movement","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Judges 20 belongs to the book’s movement from recurring deliverance stories into accounts of internal idolatry, atrocity, and civil war. The violence here is therefore part of the book’s portrayal of Israel’s deepening internal disorder, rather than a return to the earlier pattern of deliverance from outside enemies.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_020_c3afbf71586b2ec84ed6c2be"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"},{"id":"block_3","text":"One interpretive reading of Judges 19–21 understands the escalating warfare, sexual violence, collective punishment, and later forced wife-taking as evidence of social collapse, not as conduct offered for imitation. That reading helps frame the devastating outcome of this chapter within the larger closing sequence.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_020_9a53e7b768618b15580ecb75"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}